{-
   A class member's `@lazy` marker, and an implementation that does not carry it.

   # resolver: ownership

   The marker is part of the **position** and not a way of writing one: it rewrote the declared type
   to `() -> T` where the class stated it, and what it promises on top of that type is that reading
   the name runs the thunk and that no path reads it twice. So the two halves are compared
   separately - the occurrence, which a type comparison shows, and the marker, which it does not.

   An implementation that writes the thunk out and leaves the marker off has exactly the occurrence
   the class asked for and none of what it promises: nothing would force the argument where the body
   reads the name, and the at-most-once walk would not run over the body at all. One that adds a
   marker the class did not write is the same mistake from the other side - it wraps a position its
   callers pass a bare value to.

   `Class.yana` is the accepting side, and `Reject.Force.yana` is the rule over a member's body.
-}

data Gate = Gate {depth: I32}
data Shut = Shut {n: I32}
data Wide = Wide {n: I32}

class Guard(a):
  fn pick(self: a, @lazy v: I32) -> I32
  fn plain(self: a, n: I32) -> I32

-- The thunk written out with no marker, which is the position and not the promise.
instance Guard(Gate):
    fn pick(self: Gate, v: () -> I32) -> I32 = 0
    fn plain(self: Gate, n: I32) -> I32 = n

-- And a marker on a position the class left strict, which its callers pass a bare `I32` to - once
-- over a written type, where the rewrite happens and the occurrence comparison shows it, and once
-- over an inherited one, where it does not and there would be nothing left to compare.
instance Guard(Shut):
    fn pick(self: Shut, @lazy v: I32) -> I32 = 0
    fn plain(self: Shut, @lazy n: I32) -> I32 = 0

instance Guard(Wide):
    fn pick(self, v) -> I32 = 0
    fn plain(self, @lazy n) -> I32 = 0

fn main() -> I32 = 0
